To find the space, we will use a graph with the unit of measurement in the $y-$axis equal to the radius of the circles, and the $x-$axis split so that the centers of the bottom two pipes from the lower picture have coordinates (0,0) and (60,0).  The vertices of the equilateral triangle, from left to right, will then have coordinates $(0,20), (60,20),$ and $(30, 20 \sqrt3).$
Reflect the lower picture in the line $y=20$ to get the new picture below.  Using the distance formula, one can check that the higher picture has the same dimensions as the original, so the reflected lower picture and the original lower picture must tile the same amount of the upper part $20 \sqrt3$ of the oversized equilateral triangle.
